diff --git a/crates/spirv-std/README.md b/crates/spirv-std/README.md
index 9d2a8a1..ab3631a 100644
--- a/crates/spirv-std/README.md
+++ b/crates/spirv-std/README.md
@@ -38,0 +39,28 @@ pub fn main(
+## Native descriptor heaps
+
+`Image::from_resource_heap` and `Sampler::from_sampler_heap` load opaque values
+from the heaps bound by `VK_EXT_descriptor_heap`. They are unsafe because the
+caller must establish bounds, descriptor/resource validity, lifetime and
+synchronization. The returned values work with the existing image operations.
+
+Indices count native descriptors of the requested type, using
+`OpConstantSizeOfEXT` and `ArrayStrideIdEXT`. Rust's `size_of` for these opaque
+types is unrelated to the native descriptor stride. A mixed heap must place each
+descriptor at the exact byte offset represented by its typed index. These
+methods do not own or borrow resources and do not validate indices.
+
+Enable `DescriptorHeapEXT`, `UntypedPointersKHR`, `ext:SPV_EXT_descriptor_heap`
+and `ext:SPV_KHR_untyped_pointers`. Access need not be uniform, and the
+constructors never emit `Uniform` or `UniformId`. Divergent sampled-image access
+also declares `ShaderNonUniform` and `SampledImageArrayNonUniformIndexing`, with
+the applicable device feature enabled. `DescriptorHeapEXT` removes the need for
+`NonUniform` decoration, not the caller's resource and capability obligations.
+The existing sampling methods may retain that decoration when enabled.
+
+The constructors require no physical address or pointer-width policy. Combining
+them with `PhysicalPtr` memory access requires the separate physical64 target.
+CPU stubs panic without accessing a heap. The source tests cover sampled 2D
+float/integer images, repeated constructors, a fragment interface, divergent
+compute indices and optimized/qptr paths. This does not claim execution support
+for every image parameter, storage access, stage or device.
+
